# C# / Unity Standards

## Style

- Modern C#: meaningful nullability, `var` only when type is obvious
- `SerializeField` private fields for Inspector wiring; public only for true API
- Use `[Header]` / `[Tooltip]` sparingly for non-obvious inspector fields
- Prefer `readonly` / clear ownership of references after `Awake`/`Start`

## Unity lifecycle

- `Awake`: cache components, wire self
- `Start`: talk to other objects that must already exist
- `OnEnable`/`OnDisable`: subscribe/unsubscribe events (always paired)
- Keep `Update` thin: input → intent; heavy work in focused methods/systems

```csharp
// BAD: everything in Update, unclear ownership
void Update() {
  hp -= dt; Reload(); Aim(); UI();
}

// GOOD: Update orchestrates, methods have one job
void Update() {
  _input.Read();
  _motor.Tick(Time.deltaTime);
  _weapon.Tick(Time.deltaTime);
}
```

## Architecture seams (MVP → scale)

- Split **input**, **simulation**, **view/VFX** when a class grows past ~200 lines or 2 responsibilities
- **All game-design tunables** (damage, speed, FOV, RPM, sensitivity…) come from ScriptableObject configs — not hardcoded literals or ad-hoc Inspector floats on every MonoBehaviour
- MonoBehaviours hold a reference to the SO (`[SerializeField] PlayerMotorConfig _config`) and read values from it
- Prefer events/C# actions for decoupled UI and feedback; avoid circular MonoBehaviour references
- Do not introduce a full DI framework unless the project already uses one

## Safety

- Null-check external/scene references once at init; then assume valid or fail early
- Use layers/tags via constants or serialized references — no stringly-typed spam
- Physics/raycasts: name masks; document what they hit and why
